# Pulse tick: `/marshal night` — THE FLEET DRAIN (dotfiles-69qr).
#
# The estate's only CONSUMER loop. Every other pulse row produces (research,
# digests, briefs, writing) and `br ready` grows all week; this one drains it:
# it computes the night's token budget, selects fleet-MARKED beads across the
# opt-in repos, dispatches worktree builders, merges each landing under
# AGENTS.md's guarded sequence, and leaves an explainable ledger at
# ~/.local/state/harness/drain-ledger.jsonl.
#
# ⚠️ NOT INSTALLED, NOT ENABLED — DELIBERATELY (Zig's OQ1 resolution,
# 2026-08-09). The MAIDEN RUN IS SUPERVISED and hand-launched after the
# demesne cutover, at whatever hour that cutover completes; a timer is what
# this becomes once a supervised night has been watched end to end. The
# roster's marshal row records this schedule with the same caveat, and
# validate-seats.py's UNIT rule is WARN-by-default for exactly this ordering
# (you write the schedule row, THEN lay the timer down).
#
# THE MAIDEN LAUNCH, by hand, in the marshal window, with Zig watching:
#
#   ~/.agents/agents/scheduler/pulse-inject.sh --loop pulse-marshal \
#     --dir ~/dotfiles --session zig-computer --window marshal --fresh \
#     --cmd "/marshal night"
#
# (That is the ExecStart below with %h expanded and %p written out. It is not
# run by any test — running it IS the maiden run — so its flags were checked
# one by one against pulse-inject.sh's own argument parser instead.)
#
# TO INSTALL LATER, once the maiden night has been reviewed — and touch the
# stamp FIRST or Persistent= reads a never-run unit as a MISSED slot and fires
# the drain immediately, at whatever hour you happen to be installing:
#
#   touch ~/.local/share/systemd/timers/stamp-pulse-marshal.timer
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now pulse-marshal.timer
#
# --session zig-computer: ONE session per server, named for the host (Zig's
# ruling 2026-08-09 — session:window == host:seat universally).
#
# --fresh: warm process, COLD CONTEXT. A drain has no dependence on last
# night's transcript, and it is the one loop that MUST start with the whole
# window available to it — the seat molts on its own pacing threshold all
# night, and starting half-full wastes the first molt.
#
# NO --model FLAG ON PURPOSE. The pin is resolved from the roster
# (agents/seats.yml -> marshal: model: fable) by pulse-inject itself
# (dotfiles-pulse-row-model-seat-d0bk). Passing --model here would SKIP the
# roster and fork the fact. The marshal PLANS and REVIEWS on fable; the
# BUILDERS it dispatches run sonnet (or opus where a bead's `Fleet-Model:`
# marker says so) — a different model for review is the point, not an
# inconsistency. ⚠️ The pin activates on a COLD LAUNCH only: a warm `marshal`
# pane keeps whatever model it was launched with and pulse-inject logs that as
# a WARN. For the maiden run, launch into a fresh window.
#
# NO --config-dir: OQ5 (Zig, 2026-08-09) puts the marshal on the PERSONAL tap
# — no dedicated tap in v1 — which is the default, so nothing is exported.
# The budget calculator measures that same tap's spend, so this must stay in
# agreement with `tap=personal` in agents/scheduler/marshal.conf.
#
# Inspect runs: journalctl --user -u pulse-marshal.service -n 20
[Unit]
Description=Pulse tick: /marshal night (the fleet drain — the estate's overnight consumer)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=%h/.agents/agents/scheduler/pulse-inject.sh --loop %p --dir %h/dotfiles --session zig-computer --window marshal --fresh --cmd "/marshal night"
